{
  "teamId": 142,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Minnesota Twins opened the year with promise, racing to a 16-12 April on the back of timely hitting from Carlos Correa and a lineup brimming with power. But the calendar turned cruel in May and June, when the bullpen cracked at critical moments — Jovani Morán, Jhoan Duran, and Emilio Pagán all surrendered pivotal runs in high-leverage spots — and the team stumbled to a combined 24-30 over those two months. Brock Stewart's surrender of an Altuve grand slam in Houston and a rash of late-inning meltdowns left Minnesota treading water in the AL Central race, testing the faith of even the most loyal Target Field faithful.\n\nJuly became the season's hinge point. Alex Kirilloff emerged as a clutch force with back-to-back walk-off type doubles, Jorge Polanco drove in runs at crucial junctures, and Joey Gallo provided unexpected pop. The Twins went 13-11 in July and then surged to 15-12 in August, powered by Royce Lewis grand slams, Max Kepler home runs, and a rotation that was quietly keeping opponents off the board. Despite the bullpen occasionally stumbling — Griffin Jax's most harrowing outing cost them a game that looked won — the team's overall run differential told a story of a club that was simply better than its early-season record suggested.\n\nSeptember was when Minnesota announced itself. An 18-9 record, the best month of the year, carried them to the AL Central title. Jorge Polanco delivered a massive three-run single late in a pennant-race game, Willi Castro homered in a key divisional spot, and the rotation held firm. By the time October arrived, the Twins had won 35 of their final 54 games and felt like a team built for a deep postseason run. They swept the Blue Jays in the Wild Card round — Royce Lewis homering in Game 1 and Caleb Thielbar inducing a crucial double play in Game 2 — and dared to believe a World Series was within reach.\n\nThe ALDS against Houston told a different story. Yordan Alvarez and Jose Abreu punished Twins pitching with a series of crushing home runs, and the offense never truly solved a Astros staff that had been here before. Kyle Farmer's two-run homer in Game 2 offered a glimpse of hope, and Edouard Julien's solo shot in Game 3 showed the rookie's fearlessness on the big stage, but the Astros' experience and firepower proved too much. Jorge Polanco struck out to end the series, and just like that, a genuinely exciting Twins season — one that grew from doubt into division champion — came to a quiet close against October's most battle-tested franchise.",
